Task: Make newly captured camera photos appear in the BlueMind iOS composer immediately, with the same perceived speed as selecting an existing photo from Photos.

Current behavior:

* Selecting an existing image from Photos → thumbnail appears in the composer almost immediately.
* Taking a new photo with Camera → there is a noticeable delay before the captured image appears in the composer.

I want the camera flow to feel equally immediate.

Do not guess why it is slow. First profile/trace the complete camera path and identify exactly where the delay occurs.

Trace:

Camera button → camera presentation → capture → photo processing → file creation → image decoding → resizing/compression → metadata/orientation handling → attachment creation → composer state update → thumbnail rendering → background upload/preparation

Measure how long each stage takes.

⸻

The key UX requirement:

After the user confirms/takes the photo, show a local preview thumbnail inside the composer as soon as the captured image is locally available.

Do NOT make the user wait for:

* upload;
* backend processing;
* expensive compression;
* full-resolution decoding;
* metadata processing;
* conversion;
* network requests;
* AI processing

before displaying the attachment.

The UI should optimistically attach/display the captured photo first.

Any expensive preparation that does not block correctness should happen asynchronously afterward.

Conceptually:

capture → immediate local thumbnail in composer → background processing/upload preparation

instead of:

capture → process everything → wait → wait → wait → finally show thumbnail.

⸻

IMPORTANT QUALITY RULE

Do NOT solve the speed problem by permanently reducing the original image quality.

Keep two responsibilities separate:

* Composer → lightweight thumbnail for instant display.
* Actual attachment/fullscreen preview/upload → original/highest-quality captured image.

The thumbnail may be optimized for UI performance, but never replace or overwrite the original high-resolution capture with the thumbnail.

This is especially important because we are also fixing fullscreen image quality.

⸻

Performance

Avoid doing expensive image work synchronously on the main thread.

Check specifically for:

* full-resolution UIImage decoding on main thread;
* unnecessary JPEG recompression;
* repeated base64 conversion;
* unnecessary file copies;
* Photos-library round trips;
* duplicate image decoding;
* orientation correction blocking UI;
* synchronous filesystem operations;
* waiting for network/backend before updating composer state.

Move non-UI-critical expensive work off the main thread where safe.

⸻

Desired result

When I take a photo:

tap capture → confirm/use photo → thumbnail appears in BlueMind composer immediately or nearly immediately.

It should feel as fast as selecting a photo from Photos.

The user should not stare at an unchanged composer wondering whether the photo was accepted.

If some processing genuinely must continue, show the thumbnail immediately and process in the background.

⸻

Do NOT modify:

* Web behavior;
* existing Photos picker speed;
* image-preview quality;
* original image resolution;
* attachment sending behavior unless necessary to remove the blocking delay.

After fixing it, report:

1. Exact cause of the camera delay.
2. Timing before the fix.
3. Timing after the fix.
4. What work was moved off the critical UI path.
5. Confirmation that the composer thumbnail appears immediately.
6. Confirmation that the original full-resolution captured image is still preserved.
7. Confirmation that Web was not changed
